{
  "image_name": "01efeeba2046bdd69767480f7cc80b63.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed hippocampal mechanism linking wake encoding and later REM sleep. On the left, a schematic of the entorhinal cortex–hippocampal circuit shows two inputs converging on a CA1 neuron: one pathway from superficial entorhinal layers to distal dendrites and another route through DG/CA3 to more proximal dendrites.\n\nThe main panels compare two brain states. During encoding, high noradrenaline (NA) appears to bias plasticity so that proximal CA1 synapses, associated with novelty, undergo strengthening, while distal inputs carry familiarity-related information. During REM sleep, when NA is absent, the same CA1 neuron is reactivated, but plasticity is redistributed across dendritic compartments: distal synapses show LTP, whereas the previously strengthened proximal novelty-related synapses are weakened or depotentiated.\n\nThe traces beneath illustrate this process. Bursts of CA1 spiking accompany step-like changes in synaptic strength. Local field potentials from proximal and distal dendritic zones are aligned with different phases of theta rhythm, marked by peaks and troughs, suggesting phase-specific plasticity at the two input zones. Overall, the figure conveys how REM sleep may renormalize novelty-driven hippocampal traces while consolidating familiarity-related representations in CA1.",
